# SteambandRedux Core Standards

This project is a fork intended to become a Steam-ready first-person steampunk dungeon crawler. The rescue baseline is process-first: preserve original gameplay while making build, tests, licensing, and handoff truthful.

## 0. Test-Driven Development (TDD) - Mandatory
- **All functionality** must have best-practice tests before or alongside implementation.
- Use Unity framework (existing in src/tests/) for C code.
- Tests must cover usability, functionality, edge cases, regression prevention.
- Run full test suite (`ctest` or UnityTestRunner.exe) after changes.
- Add/update tests that materially reduce regression risk. Prioritize comprehensive coverage over "minimal tests". See src/tests/ for examples (Unity framework).
- Verify changes with narrowest meaningful checks first.

## 1. Security & Compliance for Legacy Codebase
- **Always analyze for security or compliance errors** on every change (buffer overflows, unsafe strcpy, null derefs, race conditions in legacy C).
- Fix vulnerabilities immediately (prefer small focused edits).
- Pay special attention to z-virt.c, files.c, util.c, main-win.c, controller*.c.
- Use modern practices: bounds checking, secure string functions where possible, proper error handling.
- For production Steam release: no secrets in repo, proper Steamworks integration, secure save handling.

## 2. Open Source & Licensing
- **Everything must be open source compliant**.
- Only use assets with permissive licenses (CC0, MIT, Public Domain). Prefer OpenGameArt.org, itch.io CC0 steampunk/Victorian pixel art tilesets/sprites.
- Document all asset sources and licenses in a LICENSES.md or ASSETS.md.
- Maintain original Angband/Steamband license compatibility (see readme.txt and source headers). The legacy not-for-profit language is an unresolved release risk; do not claim paid/Steam release clearance until documented.
- Do not commit into original repo - keep all work in this fork.
- Steam integration must respect Steamworks SDK license.

## 3. Git Workflow
- **Frequent sequential commits**: Small, focused, reversible edits. One logical change per commit.
- Use conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`.
- **Semver tagging and releases**: Tags are only for verified working versions. Do not cite tags that are not present in `git tag`.
- Work only in feature branches in this fork. Never push to original repo.
- Update [HANDOFF.md](HANDOFF.md) on significant changes.
- See git rule for details.

## 4. Controller-First Development
- Fully playable via keyboard **and** controller.
- Default mappings optimized for **Asus ROG Ally**.
- One dedicated playtester agent focuses on mobile PC gameplay, controller UX, intuitive menus.
- Leverage and extend existing controller_menu.c, controller_config_menu.c, controller.c.

## 5. Architecture & Extensibility
- Preserve core roguelike logic (cave generation, combat, items like ray guns).
- Transition to SDL2 for rendering, input, better controller/Steam support (as per plan).
- Design for moddability: JSON configs for content, easy extension for NPCs with AI/memory systems (future memorypalace).
- End goal: polished, controller-optimized, intuitive menus, easily extendible game.

## 6. Agent Team & Handoff
- Follow specialized skills for roles (playtester, licensing, security, modding, graphics).
- Update HANDOFF.md after every significant update with current state, completed work, risks, next steps.
- Agents must not overstate work. Mark features as implemented only when code is integrated, verified, and documented.
- Historical `agent-os/` content is reference only unless explicitly migrated.

**Always cite specific files, use small reversible edits, verify changes, prioritize production readiness for Steam.**
